WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.830
A polished AI answer can look finished long before it is trustworthy.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:07.023
So this class is not a tour of obvious buttons.

00:00:07.023 --> 00:00:15.322
It is the useful map underneath the screen: the app, the model, the working context, any optional tools, and the part that still belongs to you.

00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:22.299
When you open ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini, you are using a product, not touching a model by itself.

00:00:22.299 --> 00:00:25.200
The app assembles the experience around the model.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.276
It can add instructions, conversation, files, memory or tool results to the working context.

00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:32.815
The model processes what it is given.

00:00:32.815 --> 00:00:37.529
Optional tools may search, calculate, read a file or connect to another service.

00:00:37.529 --> 00:00:39.704
Then the app presents the response.

00:00:39.704 --> 00:00:44.056
Those layers can work together, but they are not the same thing.

00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:46.032
Start with the app.

00:00:46.032 --> 00:00:52.137
It is the interface, account, settings, permissions, limits, conversation history and connected capabilities around the job.

00:00:52.137 --> 00:00:59.386
Two apps can offer similar models but behave differently because their search, memory, file, privacy and account features differ.

00:00:59.386 --> 00:01:06.254
That is why a tip that works in one product, plan or setting may not work in another.

00:01:06.704 --> 00:01:09.798
The model is one trained component inside that product.

00:01:09.798 --> 00:01:15.641
It can produce words or images, interpret material, make a prediction or help choose a next action.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:19.766
Calling it only a text engine is no longer a safe shortcut.

00:01:19.766 --> 00:01:24.922
But giving the model more abilities does not make every answer current, sourced or correct.

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:27.672
Fluency is a presentation quality, not a receipt.

00:01:28.122 --> 00:01:31.625
Context is the material made available for this task.

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:37.462
It can include your request, relevant conversation, instructions, files, memory, or selected results from tools.

00:01:37.462 --> 00:01:40.964
Context is not the same thing as verified truth.

00:01:40.964 --> 00:01:49.526
A new chat also does not guarantee that nothing persisted: a product may supply saved instructions or memory according to its controls.

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:58.087
Ask what information was deliberately supplied, and leave private material out unless the job and the product's protections genuinely require it.

00:01:58.537 --> 00:02:01.526
Tools are optional capabilities the product may make available.

00:02:01.526 --> 00:02:03.186
Search is one of them.

00:02:03.186 --> 00:02:12.817
OpenAI's current help page says ChatGPT can search automatically when a question may benefit from the web, and also shows a visible Search choice in its source menu.

00:02:12.817 --> 00:02:15.805
Search can bring timely evidence into the working context.

00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:22.779
The model still has to interpret that evidence, and you still have to inspect whether the cited source supports the claim.

00:02:23.229 --> 00:02:29.193
Imagine a colleague asks whether a newly announced reimbursement rule applies to an expense submitted on Friday.

00:02:29.193 --> 00:02:35.858
If you ask only the question, the model may fill the missing rule and date with a plausible generalisation.

00:02:35.858 --> 00:02:39.015
It can sound decisive without having the operative policy.

00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:48.136
Change one meaningful variable: supply the responsible notice, or use visible search to find it, then require the answer to identify the exact effective-date sentence.

00:02:48.136 --> 00:02:51.644
The better answer is not better because it sounds cautious.

00:02:51.644 --> 00:02:56.906
It is better because the evidence entered the context and the remaining uncertainty stayed visible.

00:02:57.356 --> 00:02:59.820
A citation gives you somewhere to look.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.635
It does not prove the answer is right.

00:03:02.635 --> 00:03:05.803
When current or consequential facts matter, ask three questions.

00:03:05.803 --> 00:03:08.267
Did the product use a current source?

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:10.730
Does that source support this exact claim?

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.602
And is the source responsible and recent enough for the decision?

00:03:14.602 --> 00:03:22.697
If the answer affects money, health, rights, work or another person, verification is part of the task—not optional homework after the task.

00:03:23.147 --> 00:03:26.143
Now choose one low-risk task of your own.

00:03:26.143 --> 00:03:29.139
Write down what you want the model to produce.

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:31.469
Name the context the app must supply.

00:03:31.469 --> 00:03:34.131
Choose an optional tool only if it helps.

00:03:34.131 --> 00:03:37.127
Mark the claim or outcome that still needs checking.

00:03:37.127 --> 00:03:39.790
Then name the private information that stays out.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.784
That map is more useful than memorising one interface, because interfaces move.

00:03:43.784 --> 00:03:45.782
The jobs underneath them remain recognisable.

00:03:46.231 --> 00:03:57.632
Finish this sentence in your own words: an AI app is more than a model because… A strong answer names the context the app supplies, the optional tools it may use and the verification that still belongs to you.

00:03:57.632 --> 00:04:01.140
You do not need to know every technical detail before you begin.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:07.279
You do need to know which part is doing what, what evidence entered the job, and where your judgement still matters.

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:09.325
That is what you are looking at.
